{
  "dom_id": "step-q334217",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$*",
  "worked_solution": "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}(-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}({e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
